1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,880
If it drops right after you
buy, you doubt your timing.

2
00:00:04,330 --> 00:00:08,290
Do 10 popular buy signals really
raise your odds of success?

3
00:00:08,740 --> 00:00:14,270
Let's backtest them from 2016 with
SPY, an ETF that tracks the S&P 500.

4
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:19,280
Success means rising 5% above the buy
price; falling 2% first is a failure.

5
00:00:19,730 --> 00:00:23,130
Once decided, we sell the next day
and buy again on the next signal.

6
00:00:23,580 --> 00:00:27,020
We compare 2,000 accounts where
only the dates are random.

7
00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:31,670
First, no signal: buy on the
first trading day of each month.

8
00:00:32,120 --> 00:00:36,140
To see if signals help, we first
need a method with no signal.

9
00:00:36,590 --> 00:00:38,910
Green trades succeeded, red ones failed.

10
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:44,550
Top right is the success rate so far;
below it, the random accounts' average.

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,103
Random accounts use the same sell rule,

12
00:00:47,103 --> 00:00:49,800
with waiting days drawn
from each method's record.

13
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:56,820
At the end of 2021, the success rate was
56.1%, above the random average of 51.7%.

14
00:00:57,270 --> 00:01:01,110
But in 2022, 11 of 12 trades failed.

15
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:05,240
From 2023, it moved almost in
step with the random average.

16
00:01:05,690 --> 00:01:09,354
Result: 37 successes out of 80, 46.2%,

17
00:01:09,354 --> 00:01:13,500
slightly below the
random average of 47.1%.

18
00:01:13,950 --> 00:01:20,310
The gray band is the range covering
90% of random accounts: 41.5% to 53.2%.

19
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:23,409
Inside the band means no
different from random,

20
00:01:23,409 --> 00:01:25,720
and the first-trading-day buy was inside.

21
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:30,480
The second through fifth are trend
signals that buy as prices rise.

22
00:01:30,930 --> 00:01:33,460
They assume that once prices
start rising, they keep rising.

23
00:01:33,910 --> 00:01:36,600
Second: buy on a 20-day high breakout.

24
00:01:37,050 --> 00:01:40,510
Buy when the close beats the highest
close of the past 20 trading days.

25
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:46,960
At the end of 2019, its success rate was
58.8%, above the random average of 50.3%.

26
00:01:47,410 --> 00:01:51,720
After selling in April 2022, there
was no signal for almost three months.

27
00:01:52,170 --> 00:01:54,710
That year, 5 of 6 trades failed.

28
00:01:55,160 --> 00:02:00,440
After 20 decided trades, its success
rate never fell below the random average.

29
00:02:00,890 --> 00:02:05,710
Result: 31 successes out of 63, 49.2%.

30
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:13,920
Random average 47.1%; inside the 41.3% to
53.2% range, so we can't call it better.

31
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:18,520
Third: even without a new high, buy
if the price is up from a month ago.

32
00:02:18,970 --> 00:02:22,490
Buy when today's close is higher
than the close 21 trading days ago.

33
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:29,100
In June 2020, its success rate was 63.0%,
well ahead of the random average of 50.9%.

34
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:33,540
But in 2022, 10 of 12 trades failed.

35
00:02:33,990 --> 00:02:37,980
After selling in late February 2025,
there was no signal for over two months.

36
00:02:38,430 --> 00:02:43,500
Result: 42 successes out of 81, 51.9%.

37
00:02:43,950 --> 00:02:50,190
It was near the top of the random range
of 43.3% to 52.6%, but still inside.

38
00:02:51,190 --> 00:02:54,040
Fourth is the 20-day average
line, often drawn on charts.

39
00:02:54,490 --> 00:02:58,300
Buy on the day the close crosses
above the 20-day average from below.

40
00:02:58,750 --> 00:03:03,180
From July 2016 to January 2018,
it succeeded five times in a row.

41
00:03:03,630 --> 00:03:07,359
In December 2021, its
success rate was 64.7%,

42
00:03:07,359 --> 00:03:10,840
well ahead of the random average of 51.8%.

43
00:03:11,290 --> 00:03:14,810
Then, through July 2022, it
failed six times in a row.

44
00:03:15,260 --> 00:03:19,100
After 20 decided trades, it never
fell below the random average.

45
00:03:19,550 --> 00:03:26,550
Result: 37 successes out of 71,
52.1%, the highest of the four so far.

46
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:30,800
Still, it's inside the random
range of 40.3% to 54.3%,

47
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:32,700
so we can't call it better.

48
00:03:33,700 --> 00:03:36,020
The fifth looks at two
average lines together.

49
00:03:36,470 --> 00:03:40,550
Buy when the short 5-day average
crosses above the long 20-day average.

50
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:45,510
After selling in September 2017,
the next buy came in February 2018.

51
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:52,290
In July 2022, its success rate was
40.0%, below the random average of 48.5%.

52
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:55,220
It had just failed six times in a row.

53
00:03:55,670 --> 00:03:59,720
After that, it succeeded five
times in a row through June 2023.

54
00:04:00,170 --> 00:04:03,930
From 2024, 7 of 12 trades succeeded.

55
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:08,610
Result: 25 successes out
of 50, exactly 50.0%.

56
00:04:09,060 --> 00:04:15,280
Random average 47.4%; it was
inside the 38.6% to 56.2% range.

57
00:04:15,730 --> 00:04:18,930
It had the fewest trades of all
10, so the range was also wide.

58
00:04:19,930 --> 00:04:22,810
From the sixth on, the
signals buy when prices fall.

59
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:26,170
The idea: buy cheaper, and
it should rise more easily.

60
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:30,540
Sixth: if it falls 1% or more
in a day, buy the next day.

61
00:04:30,990 --> 00:04:34,714
In January 2019, its
success rate was 35.0%,

62
00:04:34,714 --> 00:04:38,100
well behind the random average of 46.9%.

63
00:04:38,550 --> 00:04:42,470
Because in 2018 alone,
8 of 12 trades failed.

64
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:46,040
In 2022 too, 13 of 20 failed.

65
00:04:46,490 --> 00:04:47,790
After 20 decided trades,

66
00:04:47,790 --> 00:04:50,930
it was never above the random
average, not even for a day.

67
00:04:51,380 --> 00:04:56,400
Result: 44 successes out of 95, 46.3%.

68
00:04:56,850 --> 00:05:04,200
The random average was 46.8% and the
range 40.7% to 52.8%; it was inside.

69
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:08,200
Seventh: buy when RSI is below 40.

70
00:05:08,650 --> 00:05:12,630
RSI is a number comparing the size of
gains and losses over the last 14 days.

71
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:16,030
Below 40, we treat it as
having fallen a lot, and buy.

72
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:20,116
In November 2019, its
success rate was 61.9%,

73
00:05:20,116 --> 00:05:23,510
well ahead of the random average of 49.3%.

74
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:28,490
But in the 2020 COVID
crash, 5 of 7 trades failed.

75
00:05:28,940 --> 00:05:32,970
After selling in November 2020, there
was no signal for over 10 months.

76
00:05:33,420 --> 00:05:37,230
And in 2022, 11 of 14 trades failed.

77
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:40,560
So it held stock on only 35% of days.

78
00:05:41,010 --> 00:05:45,640
Result: 30 successes out of 62, 48.4%.

79
00:05:46,090 --> 00:05:53,850
The random average was 46.7% and the
range 38.0% to 56.0%; again, inside.

80
00:05:54,850 --> 00:05:57,970
Eighth: buy when the fear
index, VIX, is 20 or higher.

81
00:05:58,420 --> 00:06:02,380
A higher VIX means more people
expect big market swings.

82
00:06:02,830 --> 00:06:05,520
This tests the saying "buy
when everyone is afraid."

83
00:06:05,970 --> 00:06:09,890
After selling in November 2016,
there was no signal for over 1 year.

84
00:06:10,340 --> 00:06:14,740
In 2020, 11 of 22 trades
succeeded, exactly half.

85
00:06:15,190 --> 00:06:21,770
In 2022, 18 of 24 failed, including
six in a row from August to October.

86
00:06:22,220 --> 00:06:27,920
From 2023, 14 of 23 trades
succeeded, back above half.

87
00:06:28,370 --> 00:06:33,090
Result: 48 successes out of 101, 47.5%.

88
00:06:33,540 --> 00:06:37,391
Almost the same as the
random average of 47.3%,

89
00:06:37,391 --> 00:06:40,340
and inside the 41.3% to 53.6% range.

90
00:06:41,340 --> 00:06:45,830
Ninth: buy when it falls 5% below
the high of the last 20 trading days.

91
00:06:46,280 --> 00:06:48,240
A dip buy aimed at short drops.

92
00:06:48,690 --> 00:06:52,880
After selling in July 2016, the
next signal came in February 2018.

93
00:06:53,330 --> 00:06:58,050
In the 2020 COVID crash,
6 of 9 trades failed.

94
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:01,700
In 2022 too, 11 of 15 failed.

95
00:07:02,150 --> 00:07:06,220
It held stock on 23% of
days, the fewest of all 10.

96
00:07:06,670 --> 00:07:10,950
Result: 29 successes out of 59, 49.2%.

97
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:18,600
The random average was 46.5% and the
range 38.0% to 55.8%; it was inside.

98
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:24,140
Last, the tenth: buy when it falls
5% below the highest close so far.

99
00:07:24,590 --> 00:07:27,070
A dip buy with a longer
view than the ninth.

100
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:32,230
This one, too, had no signal for over
1½ years after selling in July 2016.

101
00:07:32,680 --> 00:07:38,810
At the end of 2019, its success rate was
56.5%, above the random average of 49.8%.

102
00:07:39,260 --> 00:07:41,500
Because all five trades
that year succeeded.

103
00:07:41,950 --> 00:07:46,080
In the 2020 COVID crash, 6 of 10 failed.

104
00:07:46,530 --> 00:07:51,800
In 2022, 19 of 26 failed,
the most of all 10.

105
00:07:52,250 --> 00:07:57,050
From 2023, 14 of 23
succeeded, back above half.

106
00:07:57,500 --> 00:08:01,900
Result: 45 successes out of 94, 47.9%.

107
00:08:02,350 --> 00:08:10,110
The random average was 47.1% and the range
40.8% to 53.6%; this one was inside too.

108
00:08:11,110 --> 00:08:12,980
Let's look at all 10 results at once.

109
00:08:13,430 --> 00:08:18,390
Dots are each method's success rate; the
gray band covers 90% of random accounts.

110
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:22,750
The highest was buying on a cross
above the 20-day line, at 52.1%.

111
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:27,300
The lowest was buying on the first
trading day of each month, at 46.2%.

112
00:08:27,750 --> 00:08:31,300
Four of the top five were trend
signals that buy when prices rise.

113
00:08:31,750 --> 00:08:36,230
But all 10 were inside the random range,
no different from buying at random.

114
00:08:36,680 --> 00:08:39,166
Even for the top one,
the 20-day line cross,

115
00:08:39,166 --> 00:08:42,670
13% of random accounts had a
success rate that high or higher.

116
00:08:43,670 --> 00:08:46,880
Was the 2% failure line too loose?

117
00:08:47,330 --> 00:08:53,330
Make it stricter at 1%, and the success
rate for buying on any day drops to 33.6%.

118
00:08:53,780 --> 00:08:56,590
Then, only the 20-day line
cross came out above the range.

119
00:08:57,040 --> 00:09:00,030
At 1.5%, no method fell outside the range.

120
00:09:00,480 --> 00:09:03,168
These 10 years may have been special,

121
00:09:03,168 --> 00:09:06,510
so we changed the period
to 1994 through 2015.

122
00:09:06,960 --> 00:09:12,230
In this period, the 20-day line cross
was the lowest of all 10, at 33.3%.

123
00:09:12,680 --> 00:09:15,640
In this period too, all 10
were inside the random range.

124
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:17,760
Let's sum up.

125
00:09:18,210 --> 00:09:20,884
In this backtest, no
buy signal had a clearly

126
00:09:20,884 --> 00:09:23,380
higher success rate than buying at random.

127
00:09:23,830 --> 00:09:26,310
Trend signals led slightly
over these 10 years.

128
00:09:26,760 --> 00:09:30,980
But in the 22 years before, two of the
four were below the random average.

129
00:09:31,430 --> 00:09:35,830
And in years of steady decline like
2022, every signal had more failures.

130
00:09:36,280 --> 00:09:40,200
What changed the success rate most wasn't
the buy signal, but the failure line.

131
00:09:41,200 --> 00:09:44,780
These results use only
SPY, with data from 2016.

132
00:09:45,230 --> 00:09:48,583
Success and failure were
judged on closes only;

133
00:09:48,583 --> 00:09:51,580
we sold the next day and paid a 0.25% fee.

134
00:09:52,030 --> 00:09:56,020
So a few trades were successes that
lost money, or failures that made money.

135
00:09:56,470 --> 00:10:00,470
We only checked success rates; the size
of each gain or loss needs its own look.

136
00:10:00,920 --> 00:10:03,341
Interest on cash while
waiting for signals, taxes,

137
00:10:03,341 --> 00:10:05,520
currency costs and
inflation aren't included.

138
00:10:05,970 --> 00:10:09,260
This isn't investment advice; it's the
result of checking rules with code.

139
00:10:10,260 --> 00:10:14,030
If you have a hypothesis to backtest
next, tell us in the comments.

140
00:10:14,480 --> 00:10:16,030
Thanks for watching.
